{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["And I shall assert — like ״וְהַחֲרַמְתִּי אֶת עָרֵיהֶם״, ״וְהֶחֱזַקְתָּ בּוֹ״, ״וְהֶאֱבַדְתִּי חֲכָמִים מֵאֱדוֹם״, ״וְהַעֲלֵיתִי״. All of them have the sense of future tense and begin with a patach. And likewise ״וְהֶעֱבַדְתִּיךָ״, for it is the letters [i.e., the vav-ha-hiphil pattern] that cause this. But for the past tense one says: ״הֶעֱבַרְתִּי מֵעָלַיִךְ חַטָּאתֵךְ״, ״הֶעֱלִיתִיךָ״, ״הֶאֱבַדְתִּי״ — all of them open with a small patach. But with other letters one says: ״וְהִכְרַתִּי פְסִילֵיכֶם״, ״וְהִשְׁמַדְתִּי״ — all of them with a chirik, whether for the past or for the future."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "Rashbam's grammatical point: verbs whose first root letter is a guttural with a patach (vav-consecutive hiphil future) take a patach after the vav; past-tense forms of the same pattern open with a small patach (hataf-patach); other hiphil forms use chirik regardless of tense. פתחין/פתח קטן distinguishes full patach from hataf-patach."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:40:54.485228171Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}